== Chapter IX

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[#emph[Particular Directions in relation to the foregoing Case proposed. First, Consider the dangerous Symptoms of any Lust]: 1. #emph[Inveterateness.] 2. #emph[Peace obtained under it; the several ways whereby that is done.] 3. #emph[Frequency of success in its seductions.] 4. #emph[The Soul's fighting against it, with Arguments only taken from the Event.] 5. #emph[Its being attended with Judiciary Hardness.] 6. #emph[Its withstanding particular dealings from God. The State of Persons in whom these things are found.]]
]

=== III. Particular Directions

#smallcaps[THE] foregoing #emph[General Rules] being supposed, #emph[Particular Directions] to the Soul, for its #emph[guidance] under the sense of a #emph[disquieting lust] or distemper, (being the main thing I aim at) come next to be proposed. Now of these some are #emph[previous] and #emph[preparatory,] and in some of them the #emph[work] itself is contained. Of the #emph[first sort] are these ensuing:

==== First Direction: Consider the Dangerous Symptoms of the Lust

#emph[First,] Consider what dangerous #emph[symptoms] thy Lust hath attending or accompanying it. Whether it hath any deadly Mark on it or no: If it hath, #emph[extraordinary] Remedies are to be used; an #emph[ordinary] course of #emph[Mortification] will not do it.

You will say, what are these #emph[dangerous] Marks and #emph[symptoms,] the desperate #emph[Attendances] of an indwelling Lust that you intend? Some of them I shall name.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(1) #emph[Inveterateness]\; if it hath lain long #emph[corrupting] in thy Heart, if thou hast #emph[suffered] it to abide in Power and #emph[prevalency,] without attempting vigorously the #emph[killing] of it, and the healing of the #emph[wounds] thou hast received by it, for some long season, thy Distemper is dangerous. Hast thou permitted #emph[Worldliness, Ambition,] Greediness of #emph[Study,] to eat up #emph[other] Duties; the #emph[Duties] wherein thou oughtest to hold #emph[constant] Communion with God, for some #emph[long] season? Or #emph[Uncleanness] to #emph[defile] thy Heart, with #emph[vain,] and #emph[foolish,] and wicked #emph[Imaginations,] for many days? Thy Lust hath a #emph[dangerous symptom.] So was the Case with #emph[David,] Ps 38:5. #emph[My wounds stink and are corrupt, because of my foolishness.] When a Lust hath lain long in the Heart, corrupting, festering, cankering, it brings the Soul to a woeful Condition. In such a #emph[case] an #emph[ordinary] course of #emph[humiliation] will not do the work: Whatever it be, it will by this Means #emph[insinuate] itself more or less into all the Faculties of the Soul, and #emph[habituate] the Affections to its Company and society; it grows #emph[familiar] to the Mind and Conscience, that they do not startle at it as a strange thing, but are bold with it as that which they are wonted unto; yea it will get such advantage by this Means, as often-times to exert and put #emph[forth] itself, without having any notice taken of it at all; as it seems to have been with #emph[Joseph] in his #emph[swearing] by the Life of #emph[Pharaoh.] Unless some extraordinary Course be taken, such a person hath no ground in the world to expect that his #emph[latter] End shall be Peace.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[For first, How will he be able to distinguish between the long abode of an #emph[unmortified lust,] and the #emph[dominion] of Sin which cannot befall a Regenerate person? Secondly, How can he #emph[promise] himself, that it shall ever be otherwise with him, or that his #emph[lust] will cease #emph[tumultuating] and seducing, when he sees it fixed and #emph[abiding,] and hath done so for many days, and hath gone through #emph[variety] of Conditions with him. It may be it hath tried #emph[mercies] and #emph[afflictions,] and those possibly so remarkable, that the Soul could not avoid the taking special notice of them; it may be it hath #emph[weathered] out many a storm; and passed under much variety of Gifts in the Administration of the Word; and will it prove an easy thing to dislodge an #emph[Inmate] pleading a title by Prescription? Old neglected #emph[wounds] are often #emph[mortal,] always #emph[dangerous,] Indwelling Distempers grow #emph[resty,] and #emph[stubborn,] by continuance in #emph[ease] and quiet. Lust is such an #emph[Inmate,] as, if it can plead #emph[Time] and some #emph[Prescription,] will not easily be ejected. As it never dies of itself, so if it be not daily #emph[killed,] it will always gather strength.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(2) Secret Pleas of the Heart for the #emph[countenancing] of itself, and keeping up its #emph[peace, notwithstanding] the abiding of a Lust, without a #emph[vigorous] Gospel Attempt for its Mortification, is another #emph[dangerous symptom] of a deadly Distemper in the Heart. Now there be several ways whereby this may be done, I shall name some of them. As,]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. When upon #emph[Thoughts,] perplexing Thoughts about Sin, instead of #emph[applying] himself to the Destruction of it, a man #emph[searches] his Heart to see what #emph[Evidences] he can find of a #emph[good Condition, notwithstanding] that #emph[sin] and Lust, so that it may go well with him.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[For a man to gather up his #emph[Experiences] of God, to #emph[call] them to mind, to #emph[collect] them, consider, #emph[try,] improve them, is an #emph[excellent] thing; a Duty #emph[practised] by all the Saints; commended in the #emph[Old Testament] and the #emph[New.] This was #emph[Davids] work, when he #emph[communed with his own heart,] and called to remembrance the #emph[former loving kindness] of the Lord, Ps 77:6, 7, 8, 9. This is the Duty that #emph[Paul] sets us to practise, 2 Cor 13:5. And as it is in itself #emph[excellent,] so it hath #emph[beauty] added to it, by a proper Season. A time of Trial, or #emph[Temptation,] or Disquietness of the Heart about #emph[Sin,] is a #emph[picture of Silver] to set off this #emph[Golden Apple,] as #emph[Solomon] speaks: But #emph[now,] to do it for #emph[this End,] to satisfy Conscience, which #emph[cries] and #emph[calls] for another purpose, is a desperate Device of an #emph[heart] in love with Sin. When a mans #emph[Conscience] shall deal with him, when God shall #emph[rebuke] him for the sinful #emph[distemper] of his Heart, if he, instead of applying himself to get that Sin #emph[pardoned] in the Blood of Christ, and #emph[mortified] by his Spirit, shall relieve himself, by any #emph[such other Evidences] as he hath, or thinks himself to #emph[have,] and so #emph[disentangle] himself from under the #emph[yoke,] that God was putting on his #emph[neck]\; his Condition is very #emph[dangerous,] his Wound #emph[hardly] curable. Thus the #emph[Jews] under the #emph[gallings] of their own #emph[Consciences,] and the convincing preaching of our #emph[Savior,] supported themselves with this, that they were #emph[Abraham's Children,] and on that account #emph[accepted] with God; and so #emph[countenanced] themselves in all abominable #emph[wickedness] to their utter ruin.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[This is in some degree, a Blessing of a mans self, and saying that upon one account or other he shall have peace, #emph[although he adds drunkenness to thirst]\; love of Sin, undervaluation of Peace, and of all #emph[tastes] of Love from God, are enwrapped in such a Frame: Such a one plainly shows, that if he can but keep up #emph[hope] of escaping the #emph[wrath to come,] he can be well content to be Unfruitful in the world, at any Distance from God that is not final separation. What is to be expected from such an Heart?]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. By applying Grace and #emph[Mercy] to an #emph[unmortified] sin, or one not #emph[sincerely] endeavored to be #emph[mortified,] is this Deceit carried on. This is a #emph[sign] of an Heart greatly entangled with the #emph[Love] of Sin. When a #emph[man] hath secret thoughts in his Heart, not unlike those of #emph[Naaman,] #footnote[2 Kgs 5:18.] about his worshipping in the House of #emph[Rimmon]\; in all #emph[other] things I will walk with God; but in this thing, #emph[God be merciful] unto me; his Condition is sad. It is true indeed, a Resolution to this purpose, to indulge a mans self in #emph[any] sin on the account of #emph[Mercy,] seems to be (and doubtless in any course, is) altogether inconsistent with #emph[Christian Sincerity,] and is a badge of an Hypocrite, and is the turning of the Grace of God into #emph[wantonness, Jude] 4. but yet I doubt not but through the #emph[craft] of Satan, and their own remaining #emph[unbelief,] the Children of God may themselves sometimes be ensnared with this Deceit of sin; or else #emph[Paul] would never have so #emph[cautioned] them against it as he doth, Rom 6:1, 2. Yea indeed there is nothing more Natural, than for #emph[fleshly reasonings] to grow high and strong upon this account. The #emph[flesh] would fain be indulged unto upon the account of Grace: and every word that is spoken of #emph[mercy,] it stands ready to #emph[catch] at, and to pervert it to its own corrupt aims and purposes. To apply Mercy then to a sin not vigorously mortified, is to fulfill the End of the #emph[flesh] upon the Gospel.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[These and many other ways and wiles, a #emph[deceitful] Heart will sometimes make #emph[use] of, to countenance itself in its Abominations. Now when a man with his #emph[sin] is in this Condition, that there is a #emph[secret liking] of the sin prevalent in his Heart, and though his #emph[Will] be not wholly set upon it, yet he hath an imperfect #emph[velleity] towards it, he #emph[would practise] it were it not for such and such Considerations, and hereupon #emph[relieves] himself other ways than by the Mortification and Pardon of it in the Blood of Christ, that mans #emph[wounds stink and are corrupt,] and he will, without speedy Deliverance, be at the door of Death.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(3) #emph[Frequency] of #emph[success] in Sins seduction in obtaining the prevailing Consent of the Will unto it, is another dangerous #emph[symptom.] This is that I mean: When the sin spoken of gets the Consent of the #emph[Will] with some #emph[delight,] though it be not #emph[actually] outwardly #emph[perpetrated,] yet it hath success. A man may not be able upon outward Considerations to go along with Sin, to that which #emph[James] calls the #emph[finishing] of it, Jas 1:14, 15 as to the outward Acts of Sin, when yet the will of sinning may be actually obtained: Then hath it I say success. Now if any #emph[lust] be able thus far to prevail in the Soul of any man, as his Condition may possibly be very #emph[bad] and himself be #emph[unregenerate,] so it cannot possibly be very good, but #emph[dangerous.] And it is all #emph[one] upon the matter, whether this be done by the choice of the Will, or by Inadvertency; For that #emph[Inadvertency] itself is in a manner #emph[chosen.] When we are #emph[inadvertent] and negligent, where we are bound to #emph[watchfulness,] and #emph[carefulness,] that #emph[inadvertency] doth not take off from the #emph[voluntariness] of what we do thereupon; for although men do not #emph[choose] and #emph[resolve] to be #emph[negligent] and #emph[inadvertent,] yet if they #emph[choose] the things that will #emph[make] them so, they #emph[choose inadvertency] itself, as a thing may be chosen in its #emph[cause.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And let not men think that the evil of their hearts is in any measure #emph[extenuated,] because they seem for the most part to be #emph[surprised] into that consent which they seem to give unto it; for it is Negligence of their Duty in watching over their Hearts, that betrays them into that surprisal.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(4) When a man #emph[fighteth] against his sin only with #emph[Arguments] from the #emph[Issue,] or the #emph[punishment] due unto it; this is a #emph[sign] that sin hath taken great #emph[possession] of the #emph[Will,] and that in the Heart there is a #emph[superfluity of naughtiness.] Such a man as #emph[opposes] nothing to the #emph[seduction] of Sin and Lust in his Heart, but #emph[fear] of #emph[shame] among men, or #emph[Hell] from God, is sufficiently #emph[resolved] to do the #emph[sin,] if there were no #emph[punishment] attending it; which what it differs from #emph[living] in the #emph[practice] of Sin, I know not. Those who are #emph[Christs,] and are acted in their Obedience upon #emph[Gospel] Principles, have the Death of Christ, the #emph[Love] of God, the #emph[detestable] Nature of Sin, the #emph[preciousness] of #emph[Communion] with God, a deep #emph[grounded Abhorrency] of sin as #emph[Sin,] to oppose to any #emph[seduction] of Sin, to all the workings, strivings, #emph[fightings] of Lust in their Hearts. So did #emph[Joseph, How shall I do this great evil] (saith he) #emph[and sin against the Lord,] #footnote[Gen 39:9.] my good and gracious God? And #emph[Paul,] #footnote[2 Cor 5:14.] #emph[The love of Christ constrains us:] #footnote[2 Cor 7:1.] And #emph[having received these Promises, let us cleanse ourselves from all pollutions of flesh and Spirit.] But now if a man be so under the #emph[power] of his Lust, that he hath nothing but #emph[Law] to oppose it withal, if he cannot #emph[fight] against it with #emph[Gospel] weapons, but deals with it altogether with #emph[Hell] and #emph[Judgment,] which are the proper #emph[Arms] of the #emph[Law,] it is most evident, that sin hath possessed it #emph[self] of his Will and Affections, to a very great #emph[prevalency] and conquest.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Such a Person hath cast off (as to the particular spoken of) the Conduct of #emph[Renewing] Grace, and is kept from ruin only by #emph[restraining] Grace; and so far is he fallen from #emph[Grace,] and returned under the Power of the #emph[Law]\; and can it be thought that this is not a great provocation to Christ, that men should cast off his easy gentle #emph[Yoke] and Rule, and cast themselves under the Iron #emph[yoke] of the Law, merely out of indulgence unto their Lusts?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Try thyself by this also: When thou art by #emph[Sin] driven to make a #emph[stand,] so that thou must either serve it, and #emph[rush] at the #emph[command] of it into #emph[folly,] like the #emph[horse into the battle,] or make #emph[head] against it to suppress it; what doest thou say to thy Soul? What doest thou expostulate with thyself? Is this all? #emph[Hell] will be the end of this #emph[course, Vengeance] will #emph[meet] with me, and find me out; it is #emph[time] for thee to #emph[look about thee, evil] lies at the door. #emph[Paul's] main Argument to evince, that sin shall not have dominion over #emph[Believers,] is, that they are not under the #emph[Law,] but under Grace, Rom 6:14. If thy #emph[contendings] against Sin be all on #emph[legal] Accounts, from #emph[legal] Principles and #emph[motives,] what #emph[assurance] canst thou attain unto, that sin shall not have dominion over thee, which will be thy #emph[ruin?]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Yea know that this Reserve will not long hold out: if thy Lust hath driven thee from stronger Gospel #emph[Forts,] it will speedily prevail against this also; do not suppose that such Considerations will deliver thee, when thou hast voluntarily given up to thine Enemy those Helps and Means of Preservation which have a thousand times their Strength. Rest assuredly in this, that unless thou recover thyself with speed from this Condition, the thing that thou fearest will come upon thee; what #emph[Gospel] Principles do not, #emph[legal] Motives cannot do.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(5) When it is #emph[probable] that there is, or #emph[may] be somewhat of #emph[judiciary hardness,] or at least chastening Punishment in thy Lust as #emph[disquieting]\; This is another dangerous #emph[symptom.] That God doth sometimes #emph[leave] even those of his own, under the #emph[perplexing] power at #emph[least] of some Lust or Sin, to #emph[correct] them for former #emph[sins,] Negligence and Folly, I no way doubt. Hence was that #emph[complaint] of the Church, #emph[Why hast thou hardened us from the fear of thy Name,] Isa 63:17. That this is his way of dealing with #emph[unregenerate] men, no man questions. But how shall a man know whether there be any thing of Gods #emph[chastening hand,] in his being #emph[left] to the disquietment of his distemper?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Answ.] Examine thy #emph[Heart] and Ways: What was the #emph[state] and Condition of thy #emph[Soul] before thou fellest into the Entanglements of that sin which now thou so complainest of? Hadst thou been #emph[negligent] in Duties? Hadst thou lived #emph[inordinately] to thyself? Is there the #emph[guilt] of any great #emph[sin] lying upon thee #emph[unrepented] of? A new Sin may be #emph[permitted,] as well as a #emph[new Affliction] sent to bring an Old sin to remembrance?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Hast thou received any eminent #emph[Mercy,] Protection, Deliverance, which thou diddest not #emph[improve] in a due Manner, nor wast thankful for? Or hast been exercised with any #emph[Affliction,] without laboring for the appointed End of it? Or hast thou been wanting to the #emph[Opportunities] of glorifying God in thy Generation, which in his good Providence he had graciously afforded unto thee? Or hast thou #emph[conformed] thyself unto the World and the men of it, through the abounding of Temptations in the days wherein thou livest?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[If thou findest this to have been thy #emph[State, awake,] call upon God; thou art fast asleep in a storm of #emph[Anger] round about thee.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(6) When thy #emph[Lust] hath already #emph[withstood] particular dealings from God against it. This Condition is described, Isa 57:17. #emph[For the Iniquity of his Covetousness I was wroth, and smote him, I hid me and was wroth, and he went on frowardly in the way of his heart.] God had dealt with them about their prevailing Lust, and that several ways, by Affliction and #emph[Desertion.] But they held out against all: This is a sad Condition which nothing but #emph[mere sovereign Grace] (as God expresses it in the next verse,) can relieve a man in, and which no man ought to #emph[promise] himself, or bear himself upon. God oftentimes in his providential Dispensations meets with a man, and speaks particularly to the #emph[Evil] of his Heart, as he did to #emph[Joseph]'s Brethren in their selling of him into #emph[Egypt.] This makes the #emph[man] reflect on his #emph[sin,] and #emph[judge] himself in particular for it. God makes it to be the voice of the #emph[Danger, Affliction, Trouble, Sickness] that he is in, or under. Sometimes in #emph[Reading] of the Word, God makes a #emph[man stay] on something that cuts him to the Heart, and shakes him as to his #emph[present] Condition. More frequently in the #emph[Hearing] of the #emph[Word preached] (his great Ordinance for Conviction, Conversion and Edification) doth he meet with men. God often #emph[hews] men by the #emph[Sword] of his #emph[Word] in that Ordinance; strikes directly on their #emph[bosom] beloved #emph[Lust]\; startles the Sinner, makes him engage into the #emph[Mortification] and Relinquishment of the Evil of his Heart. Now if his Lust have taken such hold on him, as to enforce him to #emph[break] these #emph[bonds] of the Lord, and to cast #emph[these cords] from him; If it #emph[overcomes] these Convictions, and gets again into its #emph[old posture]\; if it can cure the wounds it so receives, that Soul is in a sad Condition.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Unspeakable are the #emph[Evils] which attend such a Frame of Heart: Every particular #emph[Warning] to a man in such an Estate, is an inestimable Mercy; how then doth he #emph[despise] God in them, who holds out against them; and what infinite Patience is this in God, that he doth not cast off such an one, and #emph[swear] in his wrath, that he shall never enter into his Rest.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[These and many other Evidences are there of a Lust that is dangerous, if not #emph[mortal.] As our Savior said of the #emph[evil Spirit,] This #emph[kind goes not out but by Fasting and Prayer]: So say I of Lusts of this #emph[kind]\; an ordinary Course of #emph[Mortification] will not do it, extraordinary ways must be fixed on.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[This is the #emph[First particular] Direction; Consider whether the Lust or Sin you are contending with, hath any of these #emph[dangerous symptoms] attending of it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Before I proceed, I must give #emph[one Caution] by the way, lest any be deceived by what hath been spoken. Whereas I say, the #emph[things] and #emph[evils] above mentioned may #emph[befall] true #emph[Believers,] let not any that #emph[finds] the same things in #emph[himself, thence] or from #emph[thence] conclude that he is a #emph[true Believer.] These are the Evils that #emph[Believers] may fall into, and be #emph[ensnared] withal, not the #emph[things] that Constitute a Believer. A man may as well conclude that he is a #emph[Believer,] because he is an #emph[Adulterer]\; because #emph[David] that was so, fell into #emph[Adultery;] as conclude it from the #emph[signs] foregoing, which are the #emph[evils] of Sin and Satan in the Hearts of #emph[Believers.] The #emph[seventh] of the #emph[Romans] contains the Description of a Regenerate man. He that shall consider what is spoken of his #emph[dark side,] of his #emph[unregenerate part,] of the indwelling Power and Violence of Sin remaining in him, and because #emph[he] finds the #emph[like] in himself, conclude that he is a #emph[regenerate] man, will be deceived in his Reckoning. It is all one as if you should #emph[argue,] A #emph[wise] man may be #emph[sick] and #emph[wounded,] yea do some things #emph[foolishly,] Therefore every one who is #emph[sick] and #emph[wounded] and does things #emph[foolishly,] is a #emph[wise] man. Or as if a #emph[silly] deformed #emph[Creature] hearing one speaking of a #emph[beautiful Person,] should, say that he had a #emph[mark] or a Scar that much #emph[disfigured] him, should conclude that because he hath himself #emph[scars,] and #emph[moles,] and #emph[warts,] that he also is #emph[beautiful.] If you will have Evidences of your being #emph[Believers,] it must be from those things that #emph[constitute] men Believers. He that hath these things in himself, may safely conclude, #emph[If I am a Believer, I am a most miserable one.] But that any man is so, He must look for other Evidences, if he will have Peace.]]
